  "review": [
    {
      "section": "time.clock",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "note": "Kinyarwanda tells time with the East African (Swahili-loan) system: saa moya = 7:00, saa sita = 12:00, saa kumi n'ebyiri = 18:00 (the file already had noon 'saa sita'). Encoded by rotating hours: ['sita','saba','munani','cyenda','kumi',\"kumi n'imwe\",\"kumi n'ebyiri\",'moya','mbiri','tatu','ine','tanu']. Patterns are additive: 'saa {h}', 'saa {h} n'igice' (half), 'saa {h} n'iminota <N>' with class-4 minute numerals (itanu, icumi, cumi n'itanu, makumyabiri, mirongo itatu n'itanu, mirongo ine ...). Noon/midnight distinguished as 'saa sita z'amanywa' / 'saa sita z'ijoro'. Doubts: 'ine' vs the also-common 'yine' for 10 o'clock; Rwandans also use a subtractive '... ibura iminota ...' form that I did not use because I am unsure of the concord; 'na robo' for quarter not used for the same reason."
    }
  ]
